# claude-env_example — runtime tuning for the installed Claude CLI binary.
#
# Copy to "claude-env" in the kanban root and edit:
#
#   cp claude-env_example "$PGAI_AGENT_KANBAN_ROOT_PATH/claude-env"
#
# This file is shell-sourced, AFTER shell-env:
#   - by every wake firing (the wake bootstrap,
#     team/scripts/lib/wake_bootstrap.sh), always — including a cron or
#     pseudocron firing, which has no interactive shell to inherit tuning
#     from. That is the reason this file exists: a value that tunes the CLI
#     binary itself has nowhere else to live that a headless wake firing can
#     reach;
#   - by other entry points through team/scripts/lib/env_bootstrap.sh ONLY
#     when PGAI_AGENT_KANBAN_ROOT_PATH is not already set in their
#     environment. The crontab templates export that variable, so a
#     cron-launched po-agent, pm-agent, overwatch-sweep or cleanup does not
#     source this file.
#
# FOUR-FILE CONCERN SEPARATION
# -----------------------------
# The kanban root carries four sourced/parsed config files, each with one
# concern. Putting a value in the wrong one is how a tuning knob turns into
# a credential-file edit, or a credential ends up readable by anything that
# greps shell-only files.
#
#   claude-env    (this file)  Tuning values the INSTALLED Claude CLI binary
#                               itself reads — provider-runtime knobs that
#                               belong to the CLI, not to the kanban.
#   shell-env                  Kanban paths, PATH adjustments, Python
#                               virtualenv activation. See shell-env_example.
#   secrets                    Credentials only: OAuth tokens, API keys.
#                               See secrets_example. Never put a tuning value
#                               here just because it is also shell-sourced.
#   kanban.cfg                 Kanban BEHAVIOUR (dashboard, chain, providers,
#                               wake bounds, per-agent model overrides). INI
#                               format, parsed — never executed.
#
# This file is OPTIONAL and shell-sourced:
#   - A fresh install.sh run seeds a claude-env from this file, every line
#     commented out, so it changes nothing until a line is uncommented. An
#     absent claude-env is a clean no-op — no error, no warning, no stdout,
#     no stderr, no change to any script's exit status.
#   - The file is shell-sourced — any syntax error fails the wake firing
#     loud, through the shared dual-channel error contract, rather than
#     silently continuing with a half-sourced file.
#     Test edits with: bash -n $PGAI_AGENT_KANBAN_ROOT_PATH/claude-env
#   - Sourcing order is fixed: shell-env first (it establishes
#     PGAI_AGENT_KANBAN_ROOT_PATH and the virtualenv), claude-env second. A
#     value set in both files resolves to whatever claude-env sets, because
#     it sources later.
#   - Never put credentials here — this file is not gitignored any more
#     strictly than shell-env is, and its sourcing path is not a disclosure
#     path by design (see below). Use secrets for anything sensitive.
#
# NO-DISCLOSURE RULE
# -------------------
# Nothing in the kanban's sourcing path for this file ever prints, echoes,
# or logs the VALUE of a variable this file sets, and never logs the file's
# contents. Naming the file's PATH in a diagnostic is fine; naming a VALUE
# from inside it is not. An operator may still put something sensitive here
# despite the guidance above — the sourcing path must not become the way
# that value leaks into a log.
#
# CALIBRATION RECORD
# -------------------
# Claude CLI version this file's tuning was calibrated against: 2.1.251
# Re-audit the entries below after every Claude CLI upgrade — a tuning
# value calibrated against one CLI version is not guaranteed to still be
# correct, or even still exist as a knob, on a later one.
#
# All lines below are commented out by default. Uncomment to use.


# --- Background-wait ceiling ---
# BASH_MAX_TIMEOUT_MS caps how long the installed Claude CLI will wait on a
# Bash tool invocation — including a backgrounded one — before it gives up
# on its own, regardless of what the kanban's watchdog would still tolerate.
# The CLI defaults this to 600000ms (10 minutes; verified against the 2.1.251
# CLI recorded in the header above via `claude --help`). The wake path
# (team/scripts/lib/wake_bootstrap.sh) derives and exports this value on
# every firing as (MAX_TASK_SECONDS + KILL_GRACE_SECONDS) * 1000 — the
# kanban's own per-task budget plus its kill grace, in milliseconds — so a
# 600s CLI default never silently truncates work the kanban's own watchdog
# (default 5400s + 30s) was still happily waiting on. It skips deriving a
# value entirely when MAX_TASK_SECONDS=0 (the per-task watchdog disabled).
#
# The wake-path derivation only fires when this variable is unset or empty
# at that point in the wake process — it never overwrites an operator value
# already sourced from this file. Uncomment and set a value here ONLY when
# the CLI-derived default is wrong for your environment (for example, a
# provider or network known to need more headroom than the kanban's own
# task budget implies); the value you set here always wins over the
# derivation. Any non-empty value switches the derivation off, so never
# uncomment a line without a real number in it.
# Read by: the installed Claude CLI (its Bash tool timeout) and the wake
# bootstrap (which leaves a value set here alone).
# Default: unset here; the wake path then derives
# (max_task_seconds + kill_grace_seconds) * 1000 — 5430000 at the shipped
# kanban.cfg values (5400 + 30) — and the CLI's own default is 600000.
# export BASH_MAX_TIMEOUT_MS="5430000"
